# Every name of the box, two upstreams each, and TLS for all of them: Caddy is the only thing on the
# box that answers 443. This file names no site of its own and is the same on every box; the sites
# are one file per instance, `/etc/caddy/instances/<name>.caddy` — `<its domain> { import pinecall
# <its port> }` — which infra/box/Makefile writes from each instance's env file on every deploy and
# takes away when the name leaves box.env's PINECALL_INSTANCES. Nothing is rendered here, and Caddy
# reads no environment.
#
# Media does NOT pass through here: WebRTC (7881/tcp, 7882/udp) and SIP (5060, 10000-10199/udp)
# reach the host's own ports directly. A proxy in front of media is a proxy inside every call.

# What a name of this box answers, whichever instance it is: a call joined from any console is the
# same WebSocket to the same SFU, and only the control plane is the instance's own. Written once
# here and imported by each site, because two copies of a routing table are two routing tables
# that will disagree. The argument is the instance's gateway port (the port of its
# PINECALL_GATEWAY_URL).
(pinecall) {
	# LiveKit's own paths, signalling included: a browser's WebSocket is upgraded by Caddy and
	# handed to the SFU, which is how the room token ever reaches it over TLS. /agent is the
	# socket a WORKER registers on — a worker on a box of its own dials wss://<this domain>/agent.
	@livekit path /rtc* /twirp* /validate* /agent*
	handle @livekit {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7880
	}

	# Everything else is the instance's control plane: the app socket, the log, /v1/*.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{args[0]}
	}
}

# One site per instance: the manifest owns this directory whole, so a file in it is an instance.
import /etc/caddy/instances/*.caddy

# Sites that are not the runtime's — the notifier's (apps/notify installs notify.caddy there) — and
# that no runtime deploy writes or takes away. A glob that matches nothing is not an error.
import /etc/caddy/conf.d/*.caddy
